WEBVTT

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.740
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department, Cal State Long Beach.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:14.380
So we are going to recap and a little bit make it quote-unquote formal what we have been doing

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.180
so far.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:21.340
We have been doing steps, now how does this all fit into design?

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:32.720
What we have been doing and we will be doing in this course is top-down design or top-down

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:45.540
design methodology. What we want to do is to capture, which means capture the design, that

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:54.260
means that you are going to describe the task precisely, describe the desired behavior of the

00:00:54.260 --> 00:01:03.300
task precisely. And usually for combinational circuits that is going to be done either using

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:31.070
language or it can be done using truth table. So capture is transcript behavior. The next thing

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:44.030
that we need to do is convert this from this abstract explanation of what we want to be done

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:51.310
to the circuit. So that is going to be called either convert or implement the circuit.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:58.680
converted from abstract to implementation or implement.

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:13.960
And this one goes from captured behavior to circuit.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.660
So how is this done?

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.040
Even if we made the description using language,

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.420
we are going to translate that description into truth table.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:32.920
So for conversion, we start from truth table.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.220
Then using truth table, we make sum of min terms.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.080
Okay, that's why we were using all of that previous class.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.400
Then from sum of min terms, we do sum of products.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:54.530
And then after that, we can further simplify.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:59.920
Sometimes you can find optimize.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.440
Well, you are going to simplify the expression

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:04.200
and simplify the expression

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.740
is going to give you more optimal circuit.

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:11.420
So we used one way of simplifying,

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:14.300
and that is knowing the properties of Boolean algebra.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.540
And once we know properties of Boolean algebra,

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.220
we know how to manipulate,

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:23.340
or we're practicing how to manipulate the expression

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:25.540
to get a simpler expression.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:30.540
Now we are going to introduce another way of doing this.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:36.700
This is called K-maps.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:43.400
K maps, K is from a name, it's Carnot's map.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:54.470
So those K maps are visual form of simplification.

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:58.990
And for them to be, well, actually,

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:01.710
because they are visual for many people,

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:06.310
they are a little bit easier to work with

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:10.990
than simplification using the laws of,

00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:12.910
or properties of Boolean management.

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:24.900
So let's start with a function that has two variables. For that we are going to make

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:45.520
two variables came up. So to be able to do that, two variables is a two by two square.

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:53.600
Now it is very very important that the variable that is on the right hand side goes here

00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:01.760
and variable that is on the left-hand side is placed here. And we have two

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:09.960
possible values for I0, we have 0 and 1, for I1 we have also 0 and 1. If you

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:27.700
look into each little cell, this cell, that cell is characterized by having I, well

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:31.420
let me make through the table, which is another.

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:37.300
So that cell is characterized having I1 value of one,

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.960
sorry, value of zero and I0 value of zero.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:48.940
And that is this part.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:53.940
Each of them, each cell can be represented by one minterm.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:06:02.850
So if you remember, this was minterm M0.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:10.210
And for M zero, you will have, when you are writing M zero,

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.650
you will say that the corresponding expression

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:17.650
is I one prime and I zero prime.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:27.040
Zero one, one zero, one one.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:31.440
So zero one, zero one intersection is here,

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.930
min term one goes here.

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:40.930
One zero, one zero, min term two,

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:49.350
and 1 1 min term 3. Well, it would have been nicer if I made this table tiny

00:06:49.350 --> 00:07:02.580
little bit bigger. So let's see if we can fix that so that I have enough space.

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:12.140
So here is min term 2, min term 3. Min term 1. What is the expression that describes

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:28.170
minter 1. We are looking at values of i1 and i0, value of i1 and i0, i1 prime, i0 without prime.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:08:02.550
Next one, minter 2, i1 because it's 1 it goes without prime, i0, 0 prime. Third one, 1 and 1,

